    title: "ADR-0047 ruling 9: offer repo-tier overlay examples when ~/hypergumbo exists (developer audience), decline recorded, never in a non-TTY"  # d034
    status: todo_hard  # d034
    priority: 3  # d034
    parent:  # d034
    tags:  # d034
    - adr_0047  # d034
    - ux  # d034
    isbefore: []  # d034
    duplicate_of: []  # d034
    not_duplicate_of: []  # d034
    pr_ref:  # d034
    description: "Two audiences, two mechanisms -- the owner separated these explicitly. ~/.config/hypergumbo is the NORMAL user and is a subcommand, not an offer (that is WI-talaz). ~/hypergumbo is a repository CHECKOUT: nobody creates one by accident, which makes its presence a deliberate signal a config directory is not. Offer once to place EXAMPLES OF REPO-TIER overlay files there.\n\nTHREE CONSTRAINTS: nothing is ever written into an analysed repository (that is how a tool's output gets committed by accident, and a repo-tier overlay should be a deliberate act by that repo's owner); a decline is RECORDED as a decision, reusing ADR-0045 ruling 8 rather than inventing a second store; never raised when stdin is not a TTY (an offer that blocks CI or an agent run is a defect).\n\nThe repo tier itself does NOT load by default -- a repo shipping an overlay that silences its own boundaries is the INV-zosun shape, arriving on a machine whose owner never opted in."  # d034
    fields: {}  # d034
- op: discuss  # 3a35
  at: '2026-08-29T01:25:06Z'  # 3a35
  by: agent  # 3a35
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 3a35
  clock: 2  # 3a35
  nonce: 3a35  # 3a35
  message: "BLOCKER FOUND AND RESOLVED BY THE OWNER, 2026-08-28, before any code.\n\nTHIS ITEM'S INSTRUCTION IS NOT SATISFIABLE AS WRITTEN. It says the decline is\n\"RECORDED as a decision, reusing ADR-0045 ruling 8 rather than inventing a\nsecond store\". The ruling-8 store REFUSES it by construction:\n\n    backend_trust.record_decision() raises ValueError for any key not in\n    BACKENDS_EXECUTING_ANALYSED_CODE, which is frozenset({'rust_analyzer'}),\n    with the rationale \"does not execute analysed-repository code, so its\n    opt-in is a preference and belongs in hypergumbo's configuration file,\n    not the trust store\" (ADR-0045 ruling 5).\n\nAnd the configuration file is the one ADR-0045 says the tool may READ and must\nNOT write -- the same precedent ADR-0047 ruling 4 rests on. So all three doors\nwere shut: the trust store refuses a UX decline, config is unwritable by the\ntool, and this item forbids a second store.\n\nOWNER'S RULING: use a SEPARATE UX-STATE RECORD, kept clearly apart from the\ntrust store. The \"second store\" prohibition is read as \"do not duplicate the\ntrust store's job\" -- a UX decline is categorically not a trust grant, and the\ntrust store's refusal is a SECURITY guard with a written rationale that should\nnot be weakened to carry a prompt preference.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION CONSEQUENCES, recorded so the ruling is not re-litigated:\n  * The record goes under $XDG_STATE_HOME/hypergumbo/, a SIBLING of trust.d,\n    never inside it and never inside the portable config home. backend_trust\n    already establishes that root and already has a test asserting the trust\n    store does not nest inside the config directory; the same shape applies.\n  * The TTY gate is on STDIN, per ruling 9's own words. cli.py's existing\n    isatty checks are on stderr, but those gate RENDERING; an offer needs an\n    ANSWER, so the stream that must be a terminal is the one the answer comes\n    from.\n  * Nothing is written into an analysed repository. Examples go to\n    ~/hypergumbo, and containment is asserted rather than promised."  # 3a35
